{
    "id": "year7-english-modals-lesson-2-certainty",
    "title": "Expressing Certainty & Possibility with Modal Verbs",
    "description": "Master modal verbs of certainty and possibility by ranking probability from 100% to 10%, analyzing subtle shifts in certainty, and completing contextual sentence challenges.",
    "duration": 30,
    "level": "Year 7",
    "slides": [
        {
            "id": "slide-1",
            "title": "The Spectrum of Probability",
            "subtitle": "From Absolute Certainty to Remote Possibility",
            "layout": "hero",
            "components": [
                {
                    "id": "certainty-intro-heading",
                    "type": "heading",
                    "props": {
                        "content": "Understanding Modal Certainty",
                        "level": 2,
                        "align": "left"
                    }
                },
                {
                    "id": "certainty-intro-paragraph",
                    "type": "paragraph",
                    "props": {
                        "content": "Modal verbs act as a 'volume dial' for certainty in English. When you select a modal verb, you communicate exactly how confident you are about an event happening—ranging from absolute certainty (**100%**) down to low possibility (**10%**).",
                        "align": "left"
                    }
                },
                {
                    "id": "probability-spectrum-table",
                    "type": "table",
                    "props": {
                        "rows": 5,
                        "columns": 3,
                        "data": [
                            [
                                "Certainty Level",
                                "Modal Verbs Used",
                                "Example Sentence"
                            ],
                            [
                                "100% Certainty",
                                "Will / Must",
                                "The sun **will** rise tomorrow. You **must** be exhausted."
                            ],
                            [
                                "70-80% Expectation",
                                "Should / Ought to",
                                "The bus **should** arrive in five minutes."
                            ],
                            [
                                "40-50% Possibility",
                                "May / Can",
                                "We **may** go to the park later today."
                            ],
                            [
                                "10-30% Low Possibility",
                                "Might / Could",
                                "It **might** snow next week."
                            ]
                        ]
                    }
                }
            ]
        },
        {
            "id": "slide-2",
            "title": "High Certainty vs. Low Possibility",
            "subtitle": "Analyzing Will, Must, Should, Might, and Could",
            "layout": "split",
            "components": [
                {
                    "id": "high-low-heading",
                    "type": "heading",
                    "props": {
                        "content": "Strong Deduction vs. Weak Speculation",
                        "level": 3,
                        "align": "left"
                    }
                },
                {
                    "id": "high-low-paragraph",
                    "type": "paragraph",
                    "props": {
                        "content": "Notice how modal strength changes based on evidence:\n- **Must** = Logical deduction based on strong proof ('Her coat is wet; it **must** be raining outside.').\n- **Might / Could** = Pure speculation without proof ('He **might** have lost his key.').",
                        "align": "left"
                    }
                },
